    "created_at": "2018-01-05T08:56:32Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-01-05T08:56:32Z",
    "body": "I think there can be three context/motivations:\r\n\r\n1. Better for users, by ensuring error object uniqueness reproducibly.\r\n2. To allow implementation flexibility, because previous \"sometimes the same object\" behavior is hard to implement reliably.\r\n3. To promote spec-conformant cross-browser implementations, by adding tests for \"error objects are always distinct\".\r\n\r\nAn alternative to this PR is \"keep the current spec, just do not care about error object identity\".\r\n\r\nThis PR is, compared to the alternative:\r\n\r\n- Better for 1. This PR anyway achieves object uniqueness while the alternative doesn't.\r\n- Neutral for 2. Both of this PR and the alternative provide a reasonable flexibility.\r\n- Neutral for 3. The additional tests for error object uniqueness test the behavior newly ensured for 1. (i.e. check whether cloning is done just before observed), and thus the test coverage of other parts of underlying algorithms remains the same. Actually, the underlying algorithms remain containing some nondeterministic behaviors and thus can't be tested reliably.\r\n\r\nI'm a little concerned for that the underlying error-to-rethrow itself remains \"sometimes the same object\", but if 1. is important, then I'm fine with this PR.\r\nKouhei suggested that 1. is (slightly) good for users, because \"sometimes the same object\" can promote user code that depends on flaky error object identity.\r\n",
